From 0c67f8ea04e7b83ce14d231a07a6dc81ba1e9ca0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:56:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 103/119] S8 metering: the camera takes the middle of the
 frame, not the average of it

decisions/s8-metering.md approach A, parts 2 and 3: the camera half.

ComputeSceneReferredEV100 now reduces the renderer's 64 bins instead of reading
a single log-mean. One walk of the CDF finds the [Metering Band Low, Metering
Band High] percentile band - 50 to 90 by default - splits the two straddling
bins linearly (which takes the quantisation from half a stop to under a tenth,
and is not optional), takes the weighted log-mean of what is inside, and asks
for the EV that lands it on 0.18.

Why a band. For a geometric mean, a fraction f of the frame at V_hot shifts the
answer by exactly f * log2(V_hot / V_bg): the sensitivity is LINEAR in area
fraction. That is why the old meter was nearly blind to the sun (0.025 % of a
50 mm frame, 0.0035 stops) and violently sensitive to the sky (half the frame,
2.65 stops at a fabricated albedo), and why no amount of clamping could have
fixed it. 50/90 discards the shadow half - which is what dragged the airfield's
ground below mid grey - and the top decile - sky, glints, the sun. Deliberately
not UE's 10/90, which exists to make Histogram agree with Basic; the mean is
the bug and we do not want to agree with it.

The target EV is the same calibration written the other way round:
EV100 = log2(L * LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE / (1.2 * 0.18)) is algebraically identical to
log2(E * LIGHT_UNIT_SCALE * 100 / (120*pi)) once L = E * 0.18 / pi is
substituted. Metering luminance rather than an albedo-divided illuminance
estimate changes what is measured, not what "correctly exposed" means.

A band that resolves entirely inside bin 0 or bin 63 is a BOUND and not a
value, and is refused; so is a result outside the plausibility window. Refused
means hold the last EV, the rule the scalar meter already had.

Auto Darken Time (0.5 s, half of Response Time) makes the smoothing asymmetric.
A symmetric tau on an asymmetric problem is what made walking out of a hangar a
three-second over-exposure: a camera stops down at once and photopic light
adaptation is a fifth of a second, while dark adaptation takes minutes. The
scene getting brighter - the picture having to darken - now takes the short
constant; the other direction keeps Response Time. 0 restores the old symmetry.

New members, new CRCs, shipped ones untouched: 'adkt' Auto Darken Time, 'mtmd'
Metering Mode, 'mtlo'/'mthi' the two percentiles.

r_HDRDebug 1 prints the histogram as a 64-character bar, log-scaled against its
own peak, with the mode, the window and the bin width; and beside it the band's
two edges in cd/m2, the exposure they were measured at, the target EV and the
applied EV. The camera owns the reduction, so the band travels back over the
post-effect bus for the overlay to print - a metering complaint stays one
screenshot to diagnose.

